[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 5, the closing chapter of Movement 1: The Chicago Workshop. Today we revisit my GOTO Chicago 2014 conversation with Ryan Gerry, alongside our earlier SCMC reflections with Jim Suchy. This episode is deeply personal for me, because it is the story of co-founding Software Craftsmanship McHenry County with Ryan Gerry and Jim Suchy fifty miles outside downtown Chicago, and how Ryan continued running SCMC for fifteen more years after I stepped away after our two-year anniversary.\n",
  "In October 2009, at the first Software Craftsmanship North America conference, I walked up to a table at lunch and introduced myself to Ryan Gerry: 'Hi, I am Mike, where are you from?' It turned out we lived fifteen minutes apart in far northwest McHenry County, where there were zero developer user groups. Right at that table, we decided to start one. We launched CDG, the Cloud Developers Group, meeting in public library rooms, Panera Bread backrooms, and upstairs at Duke's Alehouse in Crystal Lake. In 2010, we partnered with Jim Suchy from 8th Light to focus on hands-on coding katas and co-founded Software Craftsmanship McHenry County together. Ryan secured a permanent home for us at Follett Software Company, and after I stepped away following our two-year anniversary, Ryan continued running SCMC month after month.\n",
  "Here is our core inquiry for Episode 5: What sustains a local engineering community for seventeen straight years without corporate marketing budgets or paid speakers, and what does it take to hand over the torch when your own bandwidth hits a wall? A real community is not built on one founder's ego. When I stepped away after the two-year anniversary as my travel and conference schedule grew, Ryan Gerry carried SCMC forward year after year without missing a beat.\n",
  "Let us roll the GOTO Chicago 2014 conversation with Ryan Gerry (YouTube ID: gBQfB84aqW4). Listen to how Ryan describes the rhythm of those monthly kata sessions, the partnership with Follett and 8th Light, and what happens when suburban developers have a dedicated room to practice their craft together.\n",
  "What does a seventeen-year continuous room teach us in 2026? Hype cycles come and go. When Ryan and I first met in 2009, cloud computing was the brand-new wave; today it is agentic AI. The tools change every five years, but the need for a high-trust room where working engineers can sit down, test ideas against reality, and learn from peers without posturing never changes.\n",
  "Thank you for joining Episode 5 and completing Movement 1 of The Sound Above. Full transcripts, SCMC archive recordings, and our 17-year community hub are online at just3ws.com/scmc/. To help underwrite our independent compute and archive preservation, visit patreon.com/whoistechcmty. In Episode 6, we begin Movement 2: The Practice and the Dojo, starting with Corey Haines on Coderetreat and deliberate practice. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]